>> title: One-Pot Pasta
>> category: Pasta
>> tags: pasta, tomatoes, garlic, basil, one-pot, weeknight
>> counters: One Pot
>> aka: one-pan pasta, pasta cooked in its sauce, no-drain pasta, skillet spaghetti
>> servings: 4
>> time: 30 min
>> slack: narrow — there is not enough liquid to keep the strands apart, so this is stirred every couple of minutes along the bottom and it is not walk-away time; it comes off the heat looking slightly too loose, because pasta that looks right in the pan is dry at the table
>> step.1: Dry pasta goes into the sauce and cooks there, in about as much liquid as it can drink. The starch that would have gone down the drain with the boiling water stays in the pan instead, and it is what makes the sauce cling — so this is not a shortcut version of a boiled one, it is a different sauce.
>> step.2: soften the garlic 3 min
>> step.3: bring the tomatoes up, 2 min
>> step.4: simmer 12 min, stirring, until it drinks the liquid
>> step.5: beat in the cheese off the heat
>> step.6: Stir it far more often than you would a pot of boiling water — every couple of minutes, and along the bottom — because there is not enough liquid to keep the strands apart on their own. It should look slightly too loose when the heat goes off; it tightens on the way to the table.

Dry pasta goes into the sauce and cooks there, in about as much liquid as it can drink. The starch that would have gone down the drain with the boiling water stays in the pan instead, and it is what makes the sauce cling — so this is not a shortcut version of a boiled one, it is a different sauce.

Soften @garlic{6%cloves}(sliced thin), @red pepper flakes{1/2%tsp} in @olive oil{3%Tbs} in a #deep skillet{} for ~saute{3%min}.

Simmer @&(~1)garlic oil{} with @canned whole tomatoes{28%oz}(800 g; crushed by hand), @water{2 1/2%cups}(600 mL), @kosher salt{2%tsp} for ~simmer{2%min}.

Simmer @&(~1)sauce{} with @spaghetti{12%oz}(340 g; broken in half if the pan is narrow) for ~simmer{12%min}, stirring along the bottom, until the pasta is tender and the liquid has gone into it.

Beat @grated Parmesan{3/4%cup}(65 g), @basil{1/2%cup}(torn), @olive oil{1%Tbs}, @black pepper{1/2%tsp} into @&(~1)pasta{} off the heat.

Stir it far more often than you would a pot of boiling water — every couple of minutes, and along the bottom — because there is not enough liquid to keep the strands apart on their own. It should look slightly too loose when the heat goes off; it tightens on the way to the table.
